  "title": "How to Create a Workplace Violence Prevention Plan for Your Business",
  "status": "published",
  "date": "2026-03-31T00:03:25",
  "modified": "2026-03-31T02:08:03",
  "contentHtml": "\n<p>Most businesses don’t think they need a workplace violence prevention plan… until a “small” incident turns into a very big problem. And no, this isn’t just a big-corporate issue. Any business can deal with threats, harassment, fights, angry customers, or aggressive behavior. <a href=\"https://www.osha.gov/workplace-violence\" type=\"link\" id=\"https://www.osha.gov/workplace-violence\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">OSHA’s workplace violence</a> resources make it clear this is a real hazard across many settings.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>This guide walks you through a simple, step-by-step approach to workplace violence prevention, with a free template you can plug into your business and customize.</p>\n\n\n\n<script>\n(function () {\n\n  const PDF_URL = '/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Workplace-Violence-Prevention-Plan-Template.pdf';\n\n  // ── Inject styles ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  const style = document.createElement('style');\n  style.textContent = `\n    #ads-wv-overlay {\n      display: none;\n      position: fixed;\n      inset: 0;\n      background: rgba(15, 20, 40, 0.75);\n      backdrop-filter: blur(3px);\n      z-index: 99999;\n      align-items: center;\n      justify-content: center;\n    }\n    #ads-wv-overlay.ads-wv-open {\n      display: flex;\n    }\n    #ads-wv-modal {\n      background: #fff;\n      border-radius: 12px;\n      padding: 36px 32px 28px;\n      width: 100%;\n      max-width: 420px;\n      margin: 16px;\n      box-shadow: 0 24px 60px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);\n      position: relative;\n      font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', sans-serif;\n    }\n    #ads-wv-modal-close {\n      position: absolute;\n      top: 14px;\n      right: 18px;\n      background: none;\n      border: none;\n      font-size: 22px;\n      cursor: pointer;\n      color: #aaa;\n      line-height: 1;\n    }\n    #ads-wv-modal-close:hover { color: #333; }\n    .ads-wv-modal-icon {\n      width: 48px;\n      height: 48px;\n      background: #E8F0FE;\n      border-radius: 50%;\n      display: flex;\n      align-items: center;\n      justify-content: center;\n      margin-bottom: 14px;\n    }\n    .ads-wv-modal-icon svg { width: 22px; height: 22px; }\n    #ads-wv-modal h2 {\n      font-size: 19px;\n      font-weight: 700;\n      color: #0B1F3A;\n      margin: 0 0 6px;\n    }\n    #ads-wv-modal .ads-wv-subtitle {\n      font-size: 13.5px;\n      color: #666;\n      margin-bottom: 20px;\n      line-height: 1.5;\n    }\n    .ads-wv-steps {\n      display: flex;\n      gap: 6px;\n      margin-bottom: 22px;\n    }\n    .ads-wv-step {\n      flex: 1;\n      background: #f7f8fa;\n      border-radius: 8px;\n      padding: 10px 8px;\n      text-align: center;\n      font-size: 11.5px;\n      color: #555;\n      line-height: 1.4;\n    }\n    .ads-wv-step span {\n      display: flex;\n      justify-content: center;\n      margin-bottom: 6px;\n    }\n    .ads-wv-step span svg { width: 20px; height: 20px; }\n    .ads-wv-step strong {\n      display: block;\n      font-size: 11px;\n      font-weight: 700;\n      color: #0B1F3A;\n    }\n    .ads-wv-field {\n      margin-bottom: 14px;\n    }\n    .ads-wv-field label {\n      display: block;\n      font-size: 12px;\n      font-weight: 600;\n      color: #0B1F3A;\n      margin-bottom: 5px;\n      text-transform: uppercase;\n      letter-spacing: 0.04em;\n    }\n    .ads-wv-field input {\n      width: 100%;\n      padding: 11px 14px;\n      border: 1.5px solid #d1d5db;\n      border-radius: 7px;\n      font-size: 14px;\n      color: #0B1F3A;\n      box-sizing: border-box;\n      transition: border-color 0.2s;\n      outline: none;\n    }\n    .ads-wv-field input:focus {\n      border-color: #1A73E8;\n      box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgba(26,115,232,0.15);\n    }\n    .ads-wv-field input.ads-wv-error {\n      border-color: #e53e3e;\n    }\n    .ads-wv-field-err {\n      font-size: 11.5px;\n      color: #e53e3e;\n      margin-top: 4px;\n      display: none;\n    }\n    #ads-wv-submit {\n      width: 100%;\n      padding: 13px;\n      background: #0B1F3A;\n      color: #fff;\n      border: none;\n      border-radius: 8px;\n      font-size: 15px;\n      font-weight: 700;\n      cursor: pointer;\n      margin-top: 6px;\n      transition: background 0.2s, transform 0.1s;\n      letter-spacing: 0.01em;\n    }\n    #ads-wv-submit:hover:not(:disabled) {\n      background: #1A73E8;\n    }\n    #ads-wv-submit:active:not(:disabled) {\n      transform: scale(0.98);\n    }\n    #ads-wv-submit:disabled {\n      opacity: 0.65;\n      cursor: not-allowed;\n    }\n    #ads-wv-form-msg {\n      text-align: center;\n      font-size: 13px;\n      margin-top: 10px;\n      min-height: 18px;\n      color: #e53e3e;\n    }\n    .ads-wv-privacy {\n      text-align: center;\n      font-size: 11px;\n      color: #aaa;\n      margin-top: 12px;\n    }\n\n    /* Success state */\n    #ads-wv-success {\n      display: none;\n      text-align: center;\n      padding: 10px 0;\n    }\n    #ads-wv-success .ads-wv-success-icon {\n      display: flex;\n      justify-content: center;\n      margin-bottom: 14px;\n    }\n    #ads-wv-success .ads-wv-success-icon svg { width: 52px; height: 52px; }\n    #ads-wv-success h3 {\n      font-size: 18px;\n      font-weight: 700;\n      color: #0B1F3A;\n      margin: 0 0 8px;\n    }\n    #ads-wv-success p {\n      font-size: 13.5px;\n      color: #666;\n      line-height: 1.5;\n    }\n  `;\n  document.head.appendChild(style);\n\n  // ── Inject HTML ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  const overlay = document.createElement('div');\n  overlay.id = 'ads-wv-overlay';\n  overlay.innerHTML = `\n    <div id=\"ads-wv-modal\" role=\"dialog\" aria-modal=\"true\" aria-labelledby=\"ads-wv-modal-title\">\n      <button id=\"ads-wv-modal-close\" aria-label=\"Close\">&times;</button>\n\n      <div id=\"ads-wv-form-view\">\n        <div class=\"ads-wv-modal-icon\">\n          <svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0B1F3A\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><path d=\"M12 22s8-4 8-10V5l-8-3-8 3v7c0 6 8 10 8 10z\"/></svg>\n        </div>\n        <h2 id=\"ads-wv-modal-title\">Get the Free Prevention Plan</h2>\n        <p class=\"ads-wv-subtitle\">\n          Ready-to-use template. Instant download - no waiting, no email confirmation.\n        </p>\n\n        <div class=\"ads-wv-steps\">\n          <div class=\"ads-wv-step\">\n            <span><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0B1F3A\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><path d=\"M11 4H4a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v14a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h14a2 2 0 0 0 2-2v-7\"/><path d=\"M18.5 2.5a2.121 2.121 0 0 1 3 3L12 15l-4 1 1-4 9.5-9.5z\"/></svg></span>\n            <strong>Step 1</strong>\n            Fill in your name & email\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"ads-wv-step\">\n            <span><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0B1F3A\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><path d=\"M21 15v4a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2v-4\"/><polyline points=\"7 10 12 15 17 10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"3\"/></svg></span>\n            <strong>Step 2</strong>\n            PDF downloads instantly\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"ads-wv-step\">\n            <span><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0B1F3A\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><path d=\"M9 11l3 3L22 4\"/><path d=\"M21 12v7a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V5a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h11\"/></svg></span>\n            <strong>Step 3</strong>\n            Customize your plan\n          </div>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"ads-wv-field\">\n          <label for=\"ads-wv-name\">Your Name</label>\n          <input type=\"text\" id=\"ads-wv-name\" placeholder=\"John Smith\" autocomplete=\"name\" />\n          <div class=\"ads-wv-field-err\" id=\"ads-wv-name-err\">Please enter your name.</div>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"ads-wv-field\">\n          <label for=\"ads-wv-email\">Email Address</label>\n          <input type=\"email\" id=\"ads-wv-email\" placeholder=\"john@company.com\" autocomplete=\"email\" />\n          <div class=\"ads-wv-field-err\" id=\"ads-wv-email-err\">Please enter a valid email.</div>\n        </div>\n\n        <button id=\"ads-wv-submit\">Download the Free Template</button>\n        <div id=\"ads-wv-form-msg\"></div>\n        <p class=\"ads-wv-privacy\">\n          <svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#aaa\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" style=\"width:11px;height:11px;vertical-align:middle;margin-right:3px;\"><rect x=\"3\" y=\"11\" width=\"18\" height=\"11\" rx=\"2\" ry=\"2\"/><path d=\"M7 11V7a5 5 0 0 1 10 0v4\"/></svg>\n          No spam. We don't share your info.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n\n      <div id=\"ads-wv-success\">\n        <div class=\"ads-wv-success-icon\">\n          <svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#059669\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><path d=\"M9 12l2 2 4-4\"/></svg>\n        </div>\n        <h3>Your download is starting!</h3>\n        <p>\n          If it doesn't start automatically,\n          <a href=\"${PDF_URL}\" download id=\"ads-wv-manual-link\">click here</a>.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  `;\n  document.body.appendChild(overlay);\n\n  // ── Helpers ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  const $  = id => document.getElementById(id);\n  const isValidEmail = v => /^[^\\s@]+@[^\\s@]+\\.[^\\s@]+$/.test(v.trim());\n\n  function openModal() {\n    overlay.classList.add('ads-wv-open');\n    $('ads-wv-name').focus();\n    document.body.style.overflow = 'hidden';\n  }\n\n  function closeModal() {\n    overlay.classList.remove('ads-wv-open');\n    document.body.style.overflow = '';\n  }\n\n  function forceDownload(url) {\n    fetch(url)\n      .then(res => res.blob())\n      .then(blob => {\n        const a    = document.createElement('a');\n        a.href     = URL.createObjectURL(blob);\n        a.download = 'Rayven Security Protection-Workplace-Violence-Prevention-Plan.pdf';\n        document.body.appendChild(a);\n        a.click();\n        document.body.removeChild(a);\n        setTimeout(() => URL.revokeObjectURL(a.href), 5000);\n      })\n      .catch(() => {\n        window.open(url, '_blank');\n      });\n  }\n\n  function showSuccess() {\n    $('ads-wv-form-view').style.display = 'none';\n    $('ads-wv-success').style.display   = 'block';\n    forceDownload(PDF_URL);\n  }\n\n  function saveLead(name, email) {\n    const ajaxUrl = window.adsChecklist?.ajaxUrl || '/wp-admin/admin-ajax.php';\n    const nonce   = window.adsChecklist?.nonce   || '';\n\n    const body = new FormData();\n    body.append('action', 'ads_save_lead');\n    body.append('nonce',  nonce);\n    body.append('name',   name);\n    body.append('email',  email);\n    body.append('source', 'violence-prevention');\n\n    return fetch(ajaxUrl, { method: 'POST', body, credentials: 'same-origin' })\n      .then(r => {\n        if (!r.ok) throw new Error('Server returned ' + r.status);\n        return r.json();\n      })\n      .catch(err => {\n        console.error('Rayven Security Protection Violence Prevention - save failed:', err);\n        return { success: false };\n      });\n  }\n\n  // ── Form submit ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  $('ads-wv-submit').addEventListener('click', async function () {\n    const nameVal  = $('ads-wv-name').value.trim();\n    const emailVal = $('ads-wv-email').value.trim();\n    let   valid    = true;\n\n    $('ads-wv-name').classList.remove('ads-wv-error');\n    $('ads-wv-email').classList.remove('ads-wv-error');\n    $('ads-wv-name-err').style.display  = 'none';\n    $('ads-wv-email-err').style.display = 'none';\n    $('ads-wv-form-msg').textContent    = '';\n\n    if ( !nameVal ) {\n      $('ads-wv-name').classList.add('ads-wv-error');\n      $('ads-wv-name-err').style.display = 'block';\n      valid = false;\n    }\n    if ( !isValidEmail(emailVal) ) {\n      $('ads-wv-email').classList.add('ads-wv-error');\n      $('ads-wv-email-err').style.display = 'block';\n      valid = false;\n    }\n    if ( !valid ) return;\n\n    const btn = $('ads-wv-submit');\n    btn.disabled     = true;\n    btn.textContent  = 'Saving...';\n\n    const result = await saveLead(nameVal, emailVal);\n\n    if ( !result.success ) {\n      console.error('Rayven Security Protection Violence Prevention - backend response:', result);\n    }\n\n    // Always give the user the download\n    showSuccess();\n\n    btn.disabled    = false;\n    btn.textContent = 'Download the Free Template';\n  });\n\n  // ── Close handlers ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  $('ads-wv-modal-close').addEventListener('click', closeModal);\n  overlay.addEventListener('click', e => {\n    if (e.target === overlay) closeModal();\n  });\n  document.addEventListener('keydown', e => {\n    if (e.key === 'Escape') closeModal();\n  });\n\n  // ── Hook into #violence-prevention-template button(s) ────────────────────────\n  document.addEventListener('click', function (e) {\n    if (e.target.closest('#violence-prevention-template')) {\n      e.preventDefault();\n      $('ads-wv-form-view').style.display = 'block';\n      $('ads-wv-success').style.display   = 'none';\n      $('ads-wv-name').value              = '';\n      $('ads-wv-email').value             = '';\n      openModal();\n    }\n  });\n\n})();\n</script>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-workplace-violence-can-happen-anywhere-not-just-big-corporate\"><strong>Workplace violence can happen anywhere (not just “big corporate”)</strong></h2>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img fetchpriority=\"high\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"765\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" src=\"/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-plan-retail-conflict-1024x765.avif\" alt=\"Workplace violence prevention plan example showing early conflict between customer and employee in retail store\" class=\"wp-image-4657\" srcset=\"/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-plan-retail-conflict-1024x765.avif 1024w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-plan-retail-conflict-300x224.avif 300w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-plan-retail-conflict-768x573.avif 768w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-plan-retail-conflict.avif 1500w\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p>Workplace violence isn’t always headline-level chaos. Sometimes it looks like:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>A customer threatening staff over a return</li>\n\n\n\n<li>A heated argument that becomes physical</li>\n\n\n\n<li>A former employee coming back to “talk” (and refusing to leave)</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Harassment, intimidation, stalking, or repeated threats</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Someone wandering into employee-only areas like it’s a public park</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p>If you’re thinking, “Okay… that sounds like Tuesday,” that’s exactly why having a plan matters.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-what-is-a-workplace-violence-prevention-plan\"><strong>What Is a Workplace Violence Prevention Plan?</strong></h2>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"765\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" src=\"/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-plan-meeting-office-1024x765.avif\" alt=\"Workplace violence prevention plan meeting with employees reviewing safety procedures in office\" class=\"wp-image-4658\" srcset=\"/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-plan-meeting-office-1024x765.avif 1024w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-plan-meeting-office-300x224.avif 300w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-plan-meeting-office-768x573.avif 768w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-plan-meeting-office.avif 1500w\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p>A workplace violence prevention plan is a written plan that helps you:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Identify risks before they become incidents</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Prevent problems through policy, training, and physical security</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Respond properly when something happens</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Document incidents so you can improve (and protect your business)</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p>OSHA describes <a href=\"https://www.osha.gov/workplace-violence/prevention-programs\" type=\"link\" id=\"https://www.osha.gov/workplace-violence/prevention-programs\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">prevention programs</a> as a way to evaluate hazards and implement controls (the boring-sounding stuff that prevents the scary stuff).</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-what-it-includes-and-what-it-s-not\"><strong>What it includes (and what it’s not)</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p>It <strong>includes</strong>:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>A reporting procedure</li>\n\n\n\n<li>A workplace violence policy (simple, clear rules)</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Emergency response steps</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Training for employees and supervisors</li>\n\n\n\n<li>A workplace violence risk assessment process</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Recordkeeping and follow-up</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p>It’s <strong>not</strong>:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>A 40-page binder that nobody reads</li>\n\n\n\n<li>A “HR problem” only</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Something you write once and forget forever</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-free-download-workplace-violence-prevention-plan-template\"><strong>Free download: Workplace Violence Prevention Plan Template</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p>To make this easy, here’s a free resource you can use while you build your plan:</p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons is-layout-flex wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\" id=\"violence-prevention-template\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link has-background wp-element-button\" style=\"border-radius:0px;background-color:#ee8e09\">Download Workplace Violence Prevention Plan Template</a></div>\n</div>\n\n\n\n<p>If you’re in California, Cal/OSHA also provides a model <a href=\"https://www.dir.ca.gov/dosh/dosh_publications/Model-WPV-Plan-General-Industry.docx\" type=\"link\" id=\"https://www.dir.ca.gov/dosh/dosh_publications/Model-WPV-Plan-General-Industry.docx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">workplace violence prevention plan template</a> for general industry employers, which can be a useful reference when building your own</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-why-every-business-needs-a-workplace-violence-prevention-plan\"><strong>Why Every Business Needs a Workplace Violence Prevention Plan</strong></h2>\n\n\n\n<p>This isn’t about being dramatic. It’s about being prepared.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-employee-safety-in-the-workplace\"><strong>Employee safety in the workplace</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p>Your team should feel safe doing their job, period. A clear plan helps employees know what to do, what to report, and how to get help fast.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-customer-safety-liability-reduction\"><strong>Customer safety + liability reduction</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p>Incidents don’t just hurt people, they create liability, reputational damage, and messy “who did what” arguments later. A plan reduces risk by setting clear procedures and documenting response.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-faster-response-stronger-culture\"><strong>Faster response + stronger culture</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p>When people know the process, things move faster:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Who calls police</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Who handles the crowd</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Who documents</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Who supports the employees afterward</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p>That builds a calmer workplace culture and improves workplace safety and security overall.</p>\n\n\n\n<p><strong>California note (important):</strong> If you operate in <a href=\"https://ogletree.com/insights-resources/blog-posts/californias-workplace-violence-prevention-plan-law-sb-553-takes-effect-on-july-1-2024-three-weeks-until-required-compliance/\" type=\"link\" id=\"https://ogletree.com/insights-resources/blog-posts/californias-workplace-violence-prevention-plan-law-sb-553-takes-effect-on-july-1-2024-three-weeks-until-required-compliance/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">California, SB 553 (Labor Code 6401.9)</a> made workplace violence prevention planning mandatory for most employers starting July 1, 2024.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-common-workplace-violence-risks-businesses-overlook\"><strong>Common Workplace Violence Risks Businesses Overlook</strong></h2>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"765\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" src=\"/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-risk-unauthorized-access-office-1024x765.avif\" alt=\"Workplace violence risk example showing unauthorized person entering restricted employee area in office\" class=\"wp-image-4659\" srcset=\"/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-risk-unauthorized-access-office-1024x765.avif 1024w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-risk-unauthorized-access-office-300x224.avif 300w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-risk-unauthorized-access-office-768x573.avif 768w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-risk-unauthorized-access-office.avif 1500w\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p>This is where your workplace violence risk assessment earns its paycheck.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>Here are common risks businesses miss because they seem “normal”:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Angry customers (especially at closing time)</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Terminated employees returning onsite</li>\n\n\n\n<li>poor access control (anyone can walk in)</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Isolated work areas (staff alone in back rooms, yards, loading docks)</li>\n\n\n\n<li>No clear reporting process (“tell your manager” isn’t a system)</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Lack of training (“just be careful” isn’t training)</li>\n\n\n\n<li>No defined response plan (everyone improvises under stress)</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-workplace-violence-risk-assessment-what-to-look-for\"><strong>Workplace violence risk assessment: what to look for</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p>A simple way to assess risk is to review people, place, and process:</p>\n\n\n\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-people-risks\"><strong>People risks</strong></h4>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Customer-facing conflict</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Cash handling</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Late-night work</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Staffing shortages (employees working alone)</li>\n\n\n\n<li>High turnover or tense workplace dynamics</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-place-risks\"><strong>Place risks</strong></h4>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Blind spots (inside and outside)</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Poorly lit parking lots</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Unsecured side doors</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Open access to employee-only areas</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Limited visibility at reception/lobbies</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-process-risks\"><strong>Process risks</strong></h4>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Unclear reporting</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Inconsistent enforcement</li>\n\n\n\n<li>No incident logs</li>\n\n\n\n<li>No training</li>\n\n\n\n<li>No post-incident follow-up</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-how-to-create-a-workplace-violence-prevention-plan\"><strong>How to Create a Workplace Violence Prevention Plan</strong></h2>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"765\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" src=\"/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-plan-walkthrough-1024x765.avif\" alt=\"Workplace violence prevention plan walkthrough with manager inspecting office and using checklist\" class=\"wp-image-4660\" srcset=\"/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-plan-walkthrough-1024x765.avif 1024w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-plan-walkthrough-300x224.avif 300w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-plan-walkthrough-768x573.avif 768w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-plan-walkthrough.avif 1500w\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p>Here’s the practical “how to prevent workplace violence” part, without turning this into a textbook.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-1-identify-potential-threats-risk-assessment\"><strong>1) Identify potential threats (risk assessment)</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p>Start with a walkthrough:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Entrances and exits</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Parking areas</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Reception/lobby</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Back offices, stock rooms, loading docks</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Any “alone work” zones</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p>Ask:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Where could someone enter unnoticed?</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Where could an employee get cornered?</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Where would a problem escalate before anyone sees it?</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p>If you want a simple walkthrough approach for doors, lighting, cameras, and perimeter weak spots, check out our guide on <a href=\"/keeping-your-business-safe-and-successful\" type=\"link\" id=\"/keeping-your-business-safe-and-successful\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">keeping your business safe and successful</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-2-create-a-clear-reporting-procedure\"><strong>2) Create a clear reporting procedure</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p>Your reporting system should be simple enough that people actually use it.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>Define:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Who</strong> employees report to</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>How</strong> they report (phone, form, app, email)</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>What</strong> should be reported (threats, harassment, suspicious behavior, escalating conflict)</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>What happens next</strong> (who reviews, how quickly, what’s documented)</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-3-set-a-zero-tolerance-policy\"><strong>3) Set a zero-tolerance policy</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p>A workplace violence policy should be short and blunt:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>No threats</li>\n\n\n\n<li>No harassment</li>\n\n\n\n<li>No intimidation</li>\n\n\n\n<li>No physical violence</li>\n\n\n\n<li>No weapons on site (unless explicitly authorized)</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p>Also define consequences and escalation steps. People behave better when boundaries are clear.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-4-define-emergency-response-steps\"><strong>4) Define emergency response steps</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p>Create a simple “if this, then that” flow:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Immediate threat / violence:</strong> call 911</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Aggressive behavior escalating:</strong> notify manager/security, create distance, keep others safe</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Trespasser refusing to leave:</strong> call security/law enforcement as appropriate</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Medical emergency:</strong> call 911, provide first aid, document</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p>Assign roles:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Who calls</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Who guides employees/customers away</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Who locks doors</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Who documents</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-5-train-employees-and-supervisors\"><strong>5) Train employees and supervisors</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p>Training shouldn’t be a one-time slideshow nobody remembers.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>Train on:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Early warning signs (escalation cues, threats, stalking behavior)</li>\n\n\n\n<li>De-escalation basics (tone, distance, exits, not arguing)</li>\n\n\n\n<li>How to report and what details matter</li>\n\n\n\n<li>When to disengage and call for help</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p>OSHA highlights <a href=\"https://www.osha.gov/workplace-violence/prevention-programs\" type=\"link\" id=\"https://www.osha.gov/workplace-violence/prevention-programs\">training and program elements</a> as part of prevention efforts.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-6-review-and-update-the-plan-regularly\"><strong>6) Review and update the plan regularly</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p>Plans expire when reality changes:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>New layout</li>\n\n\n\n<li>New staffing pattern</li>\n\n\n\n<li>New hours</li>\n\n\n\n<li>New risks</li>\n\n\n\n<li>A near-miss incident</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p>At minimum, review it on a schedule, and immediately after any incident.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-workplace-security-measures-that-support-violence-prevention\"><strong>Workplace Security Measures That Support Violence Prevention</strong></h2>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"768\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" src=\"/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-access-control-1024x768.avif\" alt=\"Workplace violence prevention plan showing employee using access control system at restricted area door\" class=\"wp-image-4661\" srcset=\"/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-access-control-1024x768.avif 1024w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-access-control-300x225.avif 300w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-access-control-768x576.avif 768w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-access-control.avif 1500w\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p>A workplace violence prevention plan works best when it’s backed by a real workplace security plan, meaning physical measures that help prevent and manage incidents.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-access-control-visitor-management\"><strong>Access control + visitor management</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Controlled entry points</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Visitor sign-in / badges</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Restricted areas clearly marked and enforced</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Consistent ID checks where appropriate (if you want a clean policy approach, see <a href=\"/can-security-guards-check-ids-legally-california\" type=\"link\" id=\"/can-security-guards-check-ids-legally-california\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Can Security Guards Check IDs Legally in California?</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-cameras-monitoring-and-panic-buttons\"><strong>Cameras, monitoring, and panic buttons</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Cameras that actually cover entrances, lots, and high-risk areas</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Real-time monitoring (not just recording) where needed</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Panic buttons in reception / cash areas<br>If you’re exploring tech-backed coverage, <a href=\"/remote-security-monitoring-services\" type=\"link\" id=\"/remote-security-monitoring-services\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Remote Security Monitoring Services</a> pairs well with onsite security.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-lighting-layout-tweaks\"><strong>Lighting + layout tweaks</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Eliminate dark corners outside</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Improve visibility at reception</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Reduce “trap zones” where someone can get cornered<br>This is often the cheapest upgrade with the biggest payoff.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-incident-reporting-systems\"><strong>Incident reporting systems</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Simple reporting forms</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Logs for threats/harassment</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Documentation standards<br>This improves preventing violence in the workplace because patterns become visible early.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-how-security-guards-help-prevent-workplace-violence\"><strong>How Security Guards Help Prevent Workplace Violence</strong></h2>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"765\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" src=\"/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-security-guard-deescalation-1024x765.avif\" alt=\"Workplace violence prevention plan showing Rayven Security Protection security guard calmly de-escalating a situation outside commercial building\" class=\"wp-image-4662\" srcset=\"/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-security-guard-deescalation-1024x765.avif 1024w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-security-guard-deescalation-300x224.avif 300w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-security-guard-deescalation-768x573.avif 768w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-security-guard-deescalation.avif 1500w\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p>Security guards don’t replace policy and training, they support it.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-visible-deterrence-calm-de-escalation\"><strong>Visible deterrence + calm de-escalation</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p>A professional guard presence reduces the odds of someone trying something “bold.” More importantly, trained security can step in early, before a situation turns physical.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>Security helps with prevention in real ways, <a href=\"/security-guard-benefits-for-businesses\" type=\"link\" id=\"/security-guard-benefits-for-businesses\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">deterrence, quicker response, and better documentation</a> all add up.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-handling-trespassers-and-policy-enforcement\"><strong>Handling trespassers and policy enforcement</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p>A big part of prevention is keeping the wrong people out:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Former employees returning</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Aggressive individuals who were previously warned</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Unauthorized visitors entering restricted areas</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p>Compliance matters here, because the wrong response can create liability fast. If you want the plain-English version, read <a href=\"/why-hiring-a-licensed-security-company-is-the-smartest-move\" type=\"link\" id=\"/why-hiring-a-licensed-security-company-is-the-smartest-move\">Why Hiring a Licensed Security Company Is the Smartest Move You’ll Make</a> and <a href=\"/california-security-guard-laws-business-owners-ignore\" type=\"link\" id=\"/california-security-guard-laws-business-owners-ignore\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Rayven Security Guard laws business owners ignore</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-faster-incident-response-better-documentation\"><strong>Faster incident response + better documentation</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p>When something happens, speed and documentation matter:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Quick response</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Clear communication with management and law enforcement</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Incident reports with timestamps and witness notes</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p>If you’re hiring outside help, make sure the company has real supervision and reporting standards, not just a guard on a schedule (our checklist here helps: <a href=\"/how-to-choose-security-guard-company\" type=\"link\" id=\"/how-to-choose-security-guard-company\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">how to choose a security guard company</a>)</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-download-the-workplace-violence-prevention-plan-template\"><strong>Download the Workplace Violence Prevention Plan Template</strong></h2>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"765\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" src=\"/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-plan-template-download-1024x765.avif\" alt=\"Workplace violence prevention plan template download shown on clipboard and laptop in realistic office desk setup\" class=\"wp-image-4663\" srcset=\"/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-plan-template-download-1024x765.avif 1024w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-plan-template-download-300x224.avif 300w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-plan-template-download-768x573.avif 768w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-violence-prevention-plan-template-download.avif 1500w\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p>If you want the fast track, use the template and customize it to your business:</p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons is-layout-flex wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\" id=\"violence-prevention-template\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link has-background wp-element-button\" style=\"border-radius:0px;background-color:#ee8e09\">Download Workplace Violence Prevention Plan Template</a></div>\n</div>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-want-a-second-set-of-eyes-on-your-workplace-security-plan\"><strong>Want a second set of eyes on your workplace security plan?</strong></h2>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"765\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" src=\"/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-security-plan-second-set-of-eyes-walkthrough-1024x765.avif\" alt=\"Workplace security plan walkthrough with business owner and consultant reviewing office layout and potential risk areas\" class=\"wp-image-4664\" srcset=\"/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-security-plan-second-set-of-eyes-walkthrough-1024x765.avif 1024w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-security-plan-second-set-of-eyes-walkthrough-300x224.avif 300w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-security-plan-second-set-of-eyes-walkthrough-768x573.avif 768w, /wp-content/uploads/2026/03/workplace-security-plan-second-set-of-eyes-walkthrough.avif 1500w\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p>Most businesses miss things because they’re used to their environment. What feels “normal” to you can look like a security gap to an outside assessor.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>If you want help building a workplace security plan that supports your prevention policy, Security guards, patrol strategy, access control, monitoring, and reporting, <a href=\"https://www.securityandpatrol.com/\" type=\"link\" id=\"https://www.securityandpatrol.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Rayven Security Protection</a> can review your site and recommend coverage that matches your risk level.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>Next steps, depending on your business:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong><a href=\"/commercial-security\" type=\"link\" id=\"/commercial-security\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Commercial Security</a></strong> for day-to-day coverage</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong><a href=\"/office-buildings\" type=\"link\" id=\"/office-buildings\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Office Buildings / Offices</a></strong> for access control and front-desk presence</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong><a href=\"/unarmed-security\" type=\"link\" id=\"/unarmed-security\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Unarmed Security</a></strong> &amp; <strong><a href=\"/armed-security\" type=\"link\" id=\"/armed-security\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Armed Security</a></strong> based on risk</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong><a href=\"/mobile-patrol-security\" type=\"link\" id=\"/mobile-patrol-security\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Mobile Patrol Security</a></strong> for after-hours checks<br>And when you’re ready, the simplest path is your <strong><a href=\"/security-and-patrol-contact-us\" type=\"link\" id=\"/security-and-patrol-contact-us\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Contact Us</a></strong> page.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"h-final-thoughts\"><strong>Final Thoughts</strong></h2>\n\n\n\n<p>Every business should have a workplace violence prevention plan, and the best ones combine policy, training, and real physical security. OSHA and Cal/OSHA resources consistently point toward prevention programs, hazard assessment, and clear procedures as the backbone. (<a href=\"https://www.osha.gov/workplace-violence/prevention-programs?utm_source=chatgpt.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">OSHA</a>)</p>\n\n\n\n<p>You don’t need a perfect plan. You need a plan people understand, can follow under stress, and can improve over time.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>And if you want it easier: grab the template, customize it, and build from there.</p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons is-layout-flex wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\" id=\"violence-prevention-template\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link has-background wp-element-button\" style=\"border-radius:0px;background-color:#ee8e09\">Download Workplace Violence Prevention Plan Template</a></div>\n</div>\n",
